@doc """
Standard `Error` type declaration.
`Error` is an abstract type for recoverable failure values.
The names of its observation functions are ordinary function names. Reusing a
name in user code does not grant access to the internal Error representation.
Concrete failures are introduced with `deferror` and then flow through this
abstract surface, most often inside `Result<T>`.
Abstract `Error` cannot be constructed directly. Concrete error values use
this shared abstraction.

`Error` is an opaque ordinary value. It can be stored in variables, fields,
containers and closures, and passed to or returned from ordinary functions.
Putting it in `Ok(error)` remains a successful Result.

Create errors through concrete `deferror` constructors; these constructors and
`Err` support ordinary callable capture. Abstract `Error` itself cannot be
constructed or captured. A concrete Error pattern can destructure its saved Payload.

`error.kind` and `error.message` read the same information as the functions
below. `Error.kind` and `Error.message` are readonly Facet paths. These paths
remain readonly through composition and cannot be updated from any scope.
Other runtime fields are not exposed. Transport preserves the original kind,
message, Payload, location and cause without reconstructing the error.

The `deferror` header declares saved fields; constructor inputs are the leading
block arguments. A nonempty Payload requires a direct terminal construction.
Only successful `match`, `if_let`, and `if_let_then` bindings expose concrete fields.
The constructor itself returns common `Error`; transport does not grant field access.
Concrete Payload paths are readonly and remain local to the successful scope.

## REPL
xldr(1)> match List::at([1], 2) { Err(ListIndexOutOfBounds(index, length) @ e) => e.index, _ => 0 }
2
"""
@builtin type Error

impl Error {
  @doc """
  Return the concrete `deferror` kind name stored in an `Error` value.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> Error::kind(NoneError)
  "NoneError"
  """
  @builtin def kind(err: Error) -> String

  @doc """
  Test whether two failures have the same concrete declaration kind.
  Messages, Payloads, causes, and diagnostic context do not affect this comparison.
  """
  @builtin def same_kind(left: Error, right: Error) -> Boolean

  @doc """
  Return the human message stored in an `Error` value.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> Error::message(NoneError)
  "None Value."
  """
  @builtin def message(err: Error) -> String

  @doc """
  Render an `Error` into the same human-oriented multiline text used by `eprint`.
  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> Error::format(NoneError)
  "Error: NoneError: None Value."
  """
  @builtin def format(err: Error) -> String
}
